item.content:encoded:
If you have ever owned a CI pipeline and cleared a wall of red builds before standup, you might recognize this routine. A nightly run finishes with thirty-seven failed tests, and by the time anyone looks, thirty have passed on a rerun and picked up a  tag while the other seven get tickets.Nobody records why the thirty failed in the first place, so the same failures come back the next night under the same label. And it is easy to see why teams settle into this, because a rerun clears the build in minutes while finding the real cause of thirty failures could take the whole team a day.That gap is where a new class of AI models starts to look tempting. Shawn Gordon’s recent HackerNoon explainer introduces , a decision model from TypeSafe AI that writes nothing and only picks an answer. It chooses from up to 255 options or returns a yes or no probability in about half a second. TypeSafe also says those probabilities are calibrated, so an answer given at 80 percent confidence should be right about 80 percent of the time.Sorting a failed test into a product defect, environment noise, or an agent error is a three-option choice question, which makes it look like a natural fit. But when Mastra, the company behind an open-source framework for building AI agents in TypeScript, tested Jev on a related triage job, the results were poor.Jev labeled 78 of Mastra’s own GitHub issues as urgent, high, or low, using only each issue’s title and body, and only about 10 of its answers matched an engineer’s judgment. Mastra concluded that the model could not see the evidence that priority depends on, and Gordon put the risk plainly in his explainer. “A consistently wrong classifier that runs cheaply is just a faster way to tag things badly.”CI triage has the same trap, because a model can sort failures well only when the pipeline has kept the evidence that separates one category from another. So the evidence has to come first and the classifier second.Three Failure Categories Need Three Different OwnersMost pipelines record a failed test as one status, and that single status hides three kinds of failure that need different fixes from different teams., Director of Engineering at  (formerly LambdaTest), draws that line before any fix starts. “We should ideally treat these failures into three different categories,”  explains. “Whether it is a product defect, like an actual defect in the product. Or if it is environmental noise, maybe a network issue, a latency problem, a backend API that went down. Or very importantly now, since agentic pipelines are involved, whether it is an agent error.”He also gives a working definition for the third category, which is the one teams find hardest to name. “If the agent’s reasoning or actions are inconsistent, that is the point where we can clearly say it is an agent error,”  says. “We have to put them in different buckets.”Each bucket leaves a different trail, and that trail is what a classifier will eventually read. A  reproduces on the same commit under the same conditions and usually traces back to a recent change in the application.  shows up as timeouts or 5xx responses from a dependency, and it tends to hit unrelated tests in the same time window.An  happens in a healthy environment and with an unchanged commit, while the agent’s steps differ from the last passing run. So the first practical move is to write those signals down as rules for each bucket and to name an owner for each one. The feature team takes defects, the platform team takes noise, and whoever owns the prompt and the model takes agent errors.Parallel Runs Hide a Fourth Category Inside the NoiseThree buckets cover most failures, but some failures look like environment noise only because of how the suite runs., also Director of Engineering at , sees this most often when teams move from local runs to large parallel grids. “The most common and unanticipated failure is shared state across parallel workers,”  highlights. “Locally, one or two tests run in parallel, and everything works, but in the cloud with 200 parallel workers you suddenly see tests depending on specific execution order.” The usual causes are global fixtures or singletons that several tests change at once, race conditions in test data setup, and ordering dependencies that nobody noticed while the suite ran one test at a time.These failures are . And a classifier that only knows three labels will file them as environment noise, because they come and go without any change to the code. The fix is to add a fourth label and give the classifier the one signal that separates it, which is whether the test still fails with a single worker.Rerunning each failure once with one worker inside the triage job costs a few seconds and turns an ambiguous failure into a clear one. And the repair itself belongs in the test, through per-worker test data and idempotent setup and teardown, and not in the infrastructure.Capture the Agent’s Steps Along With the ResultAgent errors are the hardest bucket to fill correctly, because the evidence is in decisions the agent made and not in any log the pipeline already keeps., Director of , built his team’s approach around those decisions. His team captures a trace of every action an agent takes, with full context, in a cryptographically secure audit system. It then uses separate agent evaluators to review those traces for logic errors that have happened or might happen next.“The steps that the non-deterministic system takes to get to an output are as important to test as the outputs themselves,”  explains. “By examining a detailed trace of the steps an agent takes and the tools it leverages to generate an output, you can get deep insight into where decisions are brittle and anticipate errors before they happen.”That trace is also the missing input for automated triage. After its test,  advised letting an agent gather the evidence first and letting the classifier judge what it gathered, and CI works the same way. A triage job can attach an  to every failure before any model sees it.The bundle carries the commit, the model and prompt versions, dependency health at the start of the run, the retry outcome, and a diff of the agent’s steps against the last passing run.{
  "test": "checkout > applies promo code",
  "commit": "9f3c2e1",
  "model": "provider-model-2026-09-15",
  "prompt_version": "checkout-agent@14",
  "attempts": [
    {"status": "failed", "workers": 8},
    {"status": "passed", "workers": 1}
  ],
  "dependency_health_at_start": {
    "payments-api": "degraded",
    "inventory-api": "ok"
  },
  "network_errors": ["POST /payments 503 after 30012 ms"],
  "agent_steps_diff_vs_last_pass": [
    "skipped: open_cart",
    "added: search_product"
  ]
}
Every field in that bundle points at a bucket. A degraded payments API points at noise, a failure with eight workers followed by a pass with one points at a test defect, and a changed step sequence on a healthy environment points at an agent error. When the signals conflict, as they do in this example, the bundle shows the conflict instead of hiding it. A person reading this bundle can usually name the bucket quickly, and so can a decision model.Route Failures by Label and ConfidenceOnce the evidence exists, the classifier’s job is narrow, and keeping it narrow is what makes it safe to automate., Co-Founder and CEO of , which builds AI SRE agents for incident investigation, shows how far that labeling can go once a team has the evidence to support it. His team tracks  across its agent investigations. Those labels separate tool access problems from reasoning errors, omitted steps, and unfinished runs, because a status that only says the agent responded tells the team nothing about whether it did useful work.Most CI teams do not need 51 labels on day one, but Anver’s taxonomy shows that the label set should grow as the evidence gets richer. A decision model makes that growth cheap, because a choice question over four labels or forty costs about the same to run. The harder design question is the rule for acting on each answer. The code below sends a failure straight to its owner only when the model is confident, and puts everything else in a human triage queue.LABELS = ["product_defect", "environment_noise", "agent_error", "test_defect"]
OWNERS = {
    "product_defect": "feature-team",
    "environment_noise": "platform-team",
    "agent_error": "agent-owners",
    "test_defect": "test-authors",
}
AUTO_THRESHOLD = 0.90

def route(evidence_bundle, classify):
    """classify() wraps the team's decision model or classifier."""
    label, confidence = classify(state=evidence_bundle, options=LABELS)
    if confidence >= AUTO_THRESHOLD:
        return {"label": label, "route_to": OWNERS[label], "automatic": True}
    return {"label": label, "route_to": "human-triage", "automatic": False}
The threshold should come from the team’s own data and not from a vendor’s claim. Public write-ups on Jev note that most claims about the model come from TypeSafe itself and that no calibration results have been published yet. So run the classifier in  for two weeks, keep human labels for the same failures, and check whether answers given at 90 percent confidence are actually right about 90 percent of the time.from collections import defaultdict

def calibration_report(predictions):
    """predictions is a list of (confidence, predicted_label, human_label)."""
    buckets = defaultdict(lambda: [0, 0])
    for confidence, predicted, actual in predictions:
        bucket = min(int(confidence * 10), 9) / 10
        buckets[bucket][0] += 1
        buckets[bucket][1] += predicted == actual
    for bucket in sorted(buckets):
        total, correct = buckets[bucket]
        upper = bucket + 0.1
        accuracy = correct / total
        print(f"{bucket:.1f}-{upper:.1f}  n={total}  accuracy={accuracy:.2f}")
If the 0.9 bucket comes back at 0.70 accuracy, the model is overconfident on this team’s failures. In that case, raise the threshold or improve the bundle before letting it route anything on its own. treats the classifier as the last step of triage, not the first. “A model can sort failures faster than anyone on the team, but only if the pipeline kept what that model needs to see,” he says. “Start by labeling a month of failures by hand. Once your own engineers agree on the buckets, a classifier has something real to learn from and something real to be checked against.”The first week of this work needs no model at all. Pull the last month of failed and flaky tests, label each one by hand as a product defect, environment noise, an agent error, or a test defect, and note which piece of evidence decided the label. Then make the triage job attach those pieces of evidence to every new failure, run a decision model in shadow mode against the hand labels, and switch on automatic routing only for the labels where its confidence matches its accuracy.

Slow Postgres, part 1 of 8. The SQL and raw numbers for every test are on GitHub.You add an index on , run , and Postgres reads the whole table anyway. doesn't change the plan. Someone suggests , the plan finally shows your index, and the query gets slower.Usually the planner is right. It prices a plan by the 8 KB pages it has to read and by whether it reads them in order or jumps around between them, and once you count pages the same way, you can answer most "why is it ignoring my index" tickets yourself.One note before the numbers: the AlphaVPS link at the end is a referral link, so I may get a commission at no extra cost to you, and the netcup vouchers come from my own account.Postgres prices a plan in pages, and in my test table a status with 0.9% of the rows sits on 94% of them, so its index saves 12 page reads out of 221.On 5 million rows in RAM, the index beat a full scan up to 10% of the rows when the matches were scattered, and at every share I tried (up to 50%) when they were stored together.Read cold from disk, the scattered lookups at 0.5% and 1% of rows ran 2 to 5 times slower than a full scan, and Postgres still picked them.A 200-row table gets a Seq Scan even when 2 rows match, so a plan from a tiny test copy says nothing about production.Keep out of app code, read before , and if you only query the rare values, index only those rows.What a B-tree index storesA regular B-tree index keeps only keys, the values it is sorted by, and next to each key a TID: the row's address in the heap, which is the table's main storage.A TID is a page number plus a slot on that page. You can see it as the hidden column:SELECT ctid, id, status FROM orders WHERE status = 'chargeback' ORDER BY id LIMIT 3; ctid | id | status ---------+------+------------ (7,48) | 1000 | chargeback (14,96) | 2000 | chargeback (22,8) | 3000 | chargeback Row 1000 is slot 48 on page 7. An index lookup for returns a list of addresses like these, and Postgres then visits each of those heap pages to fetch the row and check that your transaction can see it.The second step is the one people forget. To fetch one row, Postgres has to read the whole 8 KB page it sits on.The lab: 30,000 orders on 221 pagesThe lab runs on PostgreSQL 17.11 in Docker with default settings. My numbers come from a 4-vCPU, 8 GB cloud VM, but any small VPS or a laptop will do.CREATE TABLE orders ( id bigint GENERATED ALWAYS AS IDENTITY PRIMARY KEY, created_at timestamptz NOT NULL, amount_cents int NOT NULL, status text NOT NULL );
INSERT INTO orders (created_at, amount_cents, status) SELECT now() - (30000 - i) * interval '1 minute', (random() * 10000)::int, CASE WHEN i % 1000 = 0 THEN 'chargeback' -- 30 rows, 0.1% WHEN i % 100 = 0 THEN 'failed' -- 270 rows, 0.9% WHEN i % 10 = 0 THEN 'created' -- 2,700 rows, 9% ELSE 'paid' -- 27,000 rows, 90% END FROM generate_series(1, 30000) AS i;
CREATE INDEX orders_status_idx ON orders (status); ANALYZE orders;
SELECT relpages, reltuples FROM pg_class WHERE relname = 'orders'; relpages | reltuples ----------+----------- 221 | 30000 136 rows fit on a page, so 30,000 rows take 221 pages. I picked 30,000 because samples 300 × default_statistics_target rows, which is 30,000 at the default. On this table it reads every row, so the statistics are exact and your plans should come out the same as mine.Ask for the most common value and the index goes unused:EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM orders WHERE status = 'paid';
Seq Scan on orders (cost=0.00..596.00 rows=27000 width=25) Filter: (status = 'paid'::text)
221 pages × seq_page_cost 1.0 = 221 30000 rows × cpu_tuple_cost 0.01 = 300 30000 rows × cpu_operator_cost 0.0025 = 75 --- 596 Cost has no unit you can convert to milliseconds. It only means something next to another plan's cost.The value you filter on appears nowhere in that formula. Ask for with the index paths switched off and the Seq Scan still costs 596. Only the index path's price moves with the value, so the planner is choosing between a fixed price and a sliding one.For , every one of the 221 pages holds paid rows, so the index path would read the whole heap plus the index. Force it with and you get a Bitmap Heap Scan at cost 872.How many pages each status sits onSelectivity is the share of rows that pass the filter. The usual rule of thumb goes: one row, use the index; 1%, usually; 20%, it depends; 70%, Seq Scan. Here is the same table counted by pages as well as by rows:SELECT status, count(*) AS rows, count(DISTINCT (ctid::text::point)[0]) AS pages FROM orders GROUP BY status ORDER BY rows DESC; The rule of thumb says index , and Postgres agrees: Index Scan at cost 235 against 596. Run both with EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS), though, and the index path touches 209 buffers to the Seq Scan's 221, a saving of twelve page reads.It still finished faster, 0.19 ms versus 0.93, because checking 270 rows is less CPU work than filtering 30,000. For , 30 rows on 30 pages, the index path needs 32 buffers instead of 221.Two things decide how rows land on pages. Row width sets how many fit on one, and at 136 rows per page any value much more common than 1 in 136 ends up on nearly every page. Physical order is the other. I spread evenly on purpose. If your failed orders came from one bad deploy on one afternoon, they'd sit on a few neighbouring pages and the index would look great.Timing it on 5 million rows in RAMOn 221 pages everything finishes in a couple of milliseconds, so for timings I built a bigger table: 5 million rows, 326 MB, about 120 rows per page.A column holds a random number from 0 to 999,999, so matches 1% of the rows. I loaded the same rows twice, once in random order and once sorted by , and forced each plan with the settings at shares from 0.01% to 50%. was 2 GB, which kept the whole table in memory.For scattered rows Postgres picked a bitmap scan, which collects all the TIDs first and then reads each heap page once, in page order. Up to 10% of the rows it beat the Seq Scan, 98 ms to 124. At 25% it lost, 174 against 139, and the planner still chose it. A plain Index Scan, one heap visit per row, lost from 5% on.By 10% the scattered matches sit on every single page, and the bitmap scan still won. It reads all 41,667 pages but checks only the 499,000 rows the bitmap points to, while the Seq Scan checks all 5 million. With the table in RAM a page read costs next to nothing, and the time goes into checking rows.For clustered rows the index won at every share I tried, even 50%: 123 ms against 142.The same queries with a cold cacheRAM is the friendly case. For the cold runs I restarted Postgres and dropped the OS page cache before every run, so every page came off the VM's disk.The Seq Scan got about twice as slow, 250 to 350 ms at every share. The clustered index won everywhere again, 23 ms versus 263 at 5%.The scattered bitmap scan took 748 ms at 0.5% of rows, to the Seq Scan's 349. At 1% it took 1.27 seconds against 255 ms, five times slower, and that's the plan Postgres picks.The bitmap scan at 2% read more heap pages than at 1%, 37,939 versus 29,143, and still finished more than six times faster, 190 ms against 1,275. From 2% to 10% it beat the Seq Scan again.The 2% run touched 91% of the pages, so it was close to one long sequential read. The 1% run touched 70%, with holes in between. My best guess is OS readahead, which prefetches reads that look sequential and gives up when they're full of gaps. I haven't confirmed it yet.The planner has no way to see that difference. All it has is , a single guess at how much a jump costs next to a sequential read, and it used the same guess for both runs.Seq Scans on a 200-row tableA 200-row copy shows the other end of the scale, where the table is too small for any index to win.CREATE TABLE orders_small AS SELECT * FROM orders WHERE id <= 200; CREATE INDEX ON orders_small (status); ANALYZE orders_small;
EXPLAIN SELECT * FROM orders_small WHERE status = 'failed'; Seq Scan on orders_small (cost=0.00..4.50 rows=2 width=25) Filter: (status = 'failed'::text) Only 2 of the 200 rows match, but reading two pages and checking 200 rows costs just 2 + 2 + 0.5 = 4.50. Turn off and the cheapest index plan costs 6.19, because its one index page alone is priced as a random read at . If you check plans on a 200-row test fixture, this is the arithmetic you're looking at, and it tells you nothing about the same query on 30 million rows in production."We're on SSDs, set it to 1.1." Fair, and on this table that flips the plan to an Index Scan at cost 2.77. Both versions finish in 10 microseconds or less, so on a table this size it makes no difference.A partial index instead of enable_seqscanThe one place for is a scratch session, where it shows you what the other plan would cost. It applies to every query in the session, so in app code it also overrides the plans that were right, like 596 against 872 for .If the queries you care about only ask for the rare values, say a failed-payments dashboard or a chargeback queue, index only those rows:CREATE INDEX orders_status_not_paid_idx ON orders (status) WHERE status <> 'paid'; It holds 3,000 entries instead of 30,000 and takes 5 pages, where the full index takes 28. Paid orders, 90% of the inserts here, never touch it.Postgres only uses it when it can prove your filter implies the index's , and a literal does. With only the partial index in place, a prepared under a generic plan falls back to a Seq Scan, because the planner can't prove anything about a value it hasn't seen. Generic plans get their own part later in the series.The planner's statistics in pg_statsBefore adding an index, look at the column in :SELECT null_frac, n_distinct, most_common_vals, most_common_freqs FROM pg_stats WHERE schemaname = 'public' AND tablename = 'orders' AND attname = 'status'; null_frac | n_distinct | most_common_vals | most_common_freqs -----------+------------+----------------------------------+------------------------ 0 | 4 | {paid,created,failed,chargeback} | {0.9,0.09,0.009,0.001} That's what the planner believes about as of the last . If most of your queries filter on , at 0.9, they get a Seq Scan, and no extra index will change that.A negative means a fraction of the row count. On tables bigger than the 30,000-row sample these numbers are estimates; you can raise the sample with ALTER TABLE ... ALTER COLUMN ... SET STATISTICS.The same view has a column, the planner's idea of physical order. On my 5-million-row tables it was 0.01 for the scattered copy and 1 for the clustered one, and that difference in physical order is what separates the scattered and clustered lines in both charts.Before , run the query, and the rows-versus-pages count on your own table. If the value you care about sits on most of the pages and the table doesn't stay in memory, the index won't save you reads.Run it on your own serverAll the numbers above come from one 4-vCPU AMD EPYC cloud VM with 8 GB of RAM, so yours will differ. The SQL, the scripts, the raw output and the chart code are on GitHub. Run them on your own server, and if you get a different picture or think I got something wrong, open an issue or a PR.My own projects run on netcup (VPS from €2.21 a month) and AlphaVPS (VPS from €2.54 a month). If you need a box for this, either works.For netcup I have a few €5 vouchers for new customers. Each one works once, so take one and leave the rest for the next person:36nc179418264998 36nc179418264997 36nc179418264996 36nc179418264995 36nc179418264994 36nc179418264993 Paste it into the "Redeem voucher" field at the bottom of the netcup cart. If they're all gone, message me on LinkedIn and I'll send you a new one.Good luck with your plans. Part 2 is next: how to read Postgres EXPLAIN ANALYZE.I hope you understood the instructions correctly. If you have any edits to this article, I will be waiting for your comments. If I really helped you, I kindly ask you to subscribe to my LinkedIn and provide feedback on this article.

How the scanner in Foilcase works out which printing of a trading card it is looking at: Vision feature prints for the artwork, text recognition for the collector number, and a strict rule for when to ask instead of guessing. Plus the storage underneath it: an append-only ledger and one daily price file for everyone. Foilcase is my iPhone app for scanning and pricing cards from five trading card games (App Store); the user-facing write-up covers what it does.Why artwork matching picks the wrong printBefore release I built a harness on a Mac that calls the same Vision APIs as the app: 9,903 cards indexed, 400 queries, half drawn on purpose from collector numbers that more than one print shares. Each query was a synthetic degradation of an official card image (perspective, blur, glare, colour cast, JPEG).Artwork, top-1 on shared numbersArtwork, top-1 when given the correct numberThe last row changed the design. Even with the right collector number, artwork matching picks the right print 89.5% of the time, because alternate arts and higher-rarity parallels often share one number and one illustration. No embedding separates two prints of one picture, so the app asks which print, as it asks whether a card is foil. And since five candidates nearly always include the right card, the five-card sheet is the main flow, not an error state.The embedding is VNGenerateImageFeaturePrintRequest, revision 2: 768 floats, about 9 ms an image. One option matters more than it looks:static func featurePrint(of image: CIImage) -> [Float]? {
    let request = VNGenerateImageFeaturePrintRequest()
    request.imageCropAndScaleOption = .scaleFill
    try? VNImageRequestHandler(ciImage: image, options: [:]).perform([request])
    guard let observation = request.results?.first else { return nil }
    var vector = [Float](repeating: 0, count: observation.elementCount)
    _ = vector.withUnsafeMutableBytes { observation.data.copyBytes(to: $0) }
    return vector
}

static let artworkMatchingAvailable: Bool = {
    let probe = CIImage(color: .gray).cropped(to: CGRect(x: 0, y: 0, width: 64, height: 90))
    return featurePrint(of: probe) != nil
}()
The default, , cuts the top and bottom off a tall card: 82.4% against 93.2% for  on the same 1,295-card index. The probe exists because feature prints fail in the Simulator ("Failed to create espresso context"). The app checks once and falls back to the collector number alone, as it would on any device without a usable neural engine.Distance is not a confidence scoreThe obvious next step is a distance threshold. Measured, it says almost nothing: median distance was 0.450 when the top match was right and 0.420 when it was wrong. But distance does tell whether there is a card at all: photographed cards sat at a median of 0.459, a phone pointed at a laptop at 0.966. I found that out when the scanner, with no ceiling, turned a browser window into twenty-six confident entries. The ceiling is now 0.80, which keeps 99% of photographed cards and rejects 89% of everything else.Reading the collector numberThe number is unambiguous whenever it is legible, so most of the work went into legibility. Between a handheld frame and a sharp one, the artwork gained 5.6 points and the collector number 46 (32.8% read against 78.5%). The gate is the variance of the Laplacian on a greyscale buffer of the rectified card. My first version chained Core Image filters, a Laplacian then a multiply blend, and returned negative variances: blend modes clamp and premultiply, they are not arithmetic.Don't ask Vision to find the card. On iOS, VNDetectDocumentSegmentationRequest returned the card's text box at 0.96 confidence, and VNDetectRectanglesRequest found artwork panels, never the border. So the scanner draws a card-shaped guide, tries three crops around it and keeps whichever embeds closest to the index.Read in passes, at full size. Downscaling crops is how my first benchmark reported an OCR accuracy of zero.  runs over the whole card, then the top and bottom 22% enlarged 2.5 times, then three corners at 4 times if nothing turned up:private func scan(_ image: CIImage, into found: inout Set<String>, minimumTextHeight: Float = 0.008) {
    let request = VNRecognizeTextRequest()
    request.recognitionLevel = .accurate
    request.usesLanguageCorrection = false   // these are codes, not words
    request.recognitionLanguages = ["en-US"]
    request.minimumTextHeight = minimumTextHeight   // the default floor skips the number
    try? VNImageRequestHandler(ciImage: image, options: [:]).perform([request])

    for observation in request.results ?? [] {
        for candidate in observation.topCandidates(2) {
            found.formUnion(PrintedNumber.extract(from: candidate.string))
        }
    }
}
The recognizer swaps look-alikes (S and 5, O and 0, B and 8), so readings are folded before comparing. That tolerance is what kept producing wrong additions. The fix was two keys: a loose one, padding removed, for  candidates, and a literal one, padding and variant marks kept, for  without asking. "45/195" fits a catalog "045/195", but it is also what "145/195" looks like with its first digit lost.When the scanner adds and when it asksAdd without asking only when the artwork's first choice (or a card it can't tell apart from it) and a reading that spells that card's number literally name the same card, and nothing the artwork can't rule out shares that number. Everything else is the sheet. Trimmed from :func blocks(_ card: CardRef) -> Bool {
    guard card.id != winner.id else { return false }
    guard baseName(card) != baseName(winner.card) else { return true }   // same card, other print
    guard let margin = policy.distinctParallelMargin, let mine = winner.distance,
          let theirs = distances[card.id] else { return true }
    return theirs < mine * (1 + margin)
}
if parallels.contains(where: blocks) { return .ambiguous(candidates: offered, reason: .parallelPrints, numbers: numbers) }
let artworkAgrees = winner.id == candidates[0].id
    || artworkTies(candidates[0], winner, policy.minimumRelativeMargin)
let byNumber = winner.numberMatched && artworkAgrees
    && (spelled(winner.card, literals) || unpadded)
guard byNumber else {
    return .ambiguous(candidates: ordered, reason: winner.numberMatched ? .numberDisagrees : .lowConfidence, numbers: numbers)
}
return .identified(winner)
A different card sharing the number stops blocking only if the artwork puts it at least 15% farther away.  lets an old card printed "18/130" match a catalog "018/130", but only when it lands on the artwork's own first pick.The rule took three rounds of adversarial review to reach, each reported bug reproduced against the real catalogs. The first confirmed 33 defects; the second found 12 new ones, 11 introduced by the fixes. As my commit message put it, a comparison built to  candidates was also allowed to  one. Adding on artwork margin alone went too: a card missing from the index still has a nearest neighbour that can be clearly ahead and wrong.Before a silent add, a second text request also counts Han, kana and hangul characters, because artwork and number are identical across languages and each catalog holds one. English cards read 0 to 3, Japanese ones 37 to 188; the cut-off is 8.Then I measured on 529 real photographs of cards, labelled against the catalog, with all five games installed (62,331 cards in one index) and the app's recognition code running on a Mac. Every silent add was checked by eye:Added without asking, wrong cardAdded without asking, right cardRight card added, or first in the sheetThe gains came from the photos: a picked photo now skips the quality gates, since no better frame is coming; the number is read from the loosest crop when the tightest cut it off; and each crop is also embedded slightly blurred, because a holo's sparkle pulled matches away from the flat scans in the index (right card first: 377 to 397). The live camera path is still unmeasured; for that, the app keeps an on-device calibration log of each attempt and the card the user actually picked.Keeping a 27,000-card index on the phoneEach game's index downloads separately, quantised to int8 with one scale per pack. On queries perturbed to the distance a handheld photo sits at, int8 and float16 returned the same card 99.8% of the time, for half the bytes. There's no approximate nearest-neighbour library: in the Mac harness, a brute-force scan of the largest index, 26,978 vectors, takes 1.6 ms widened to , but holds 83 MB. So the app memory-maps the int8 bytes and converts 512 rows at a time with  and : 5.6 ms, nothing resident. The scan runs only on a steady frame, so four milliseconds is invisible and 83 MB isn't. A pack from a different Vision revision is refused; its distances live in another space.The index is built on a Mac and lags the catalog, so a clearly read number naming a card the artwork never offered goes to the top of the sheet, asked about and never added.A ledger that never overwrites, and one price file for everyoneThe collection is an append-only  table plus an  table derived from it in the same transaction. Removal is a tombstone;  replays the log. Replay needs an order every device agrees on, and the middle term of the sort key is a bug fix: the scanner adds a card and sets its finish within one millisecond, and a random event id sometimes sorted the edit first./// Creation sorts before every edit to the same item at the same instant.
var replayRank: Int {
    if case .added = self { return 0 }
    return 1
}

for record in records.sorted(by: { ($0.timestamp, $0.event.replayRank, $0.eventID)
                                 < ($1.timestamp, $1.event.replayRank, $1.eventID) }) {
It paid off in October. The upstream catalog renamed sets, card ids include the set name, and 1,323 cards in one game got new ids overnight, 222 more across the others. Copies saved under old ids dropped off the collection screen. The fix is an alias table read in memory; the log stays as written.Prices are one binary file per game, the same bytes for every user, so a request says nothing about what anyone owns. Names are content-addressed and the manifest is written last, so a client never sees half an update. Prices are integer cents, with a sentinel so a $0.00 promo isn't an unpriced card. Each carries the day it was captured, and past PriceProvenance.staleAfterDays (7) the portfolio headline says "These prices are 2 weeks old" instead of presenting an old total as current.How do you tell apart card printings that share the same artwork?Mostly not from the image: in my pre-release harness, artwork matching picked the right print 89.5% of the time even when given the correct collector number. Foilcase reads the number with Vision text recognition and, when other prints share it, shows the candidates and asks.Is VNGenerateImageFeaturePrintRequest good enough for on-device image recognition?For retrieval, yes, with no model to ship: the right card was in the top five 99.8% of the time on sharp synthetic frames of a 9,903-card index. Use , and test on a device, because it doesn't run in the Simulator.Do I need an approximate nearest neighbour library for on-device image search?Not at tens of thousands of vectors. A brute-force Accelerate scan over 26,978 feature prints took 1.6 ms on a Mac in Float, or 5.6 ms as memory-mapped int8, which avoids holding 83 MB in memory.Can embedding distance be used as a confidence score?Not between similar images: median distance was 0.450 for correct matches and 0.420 for wrong ones. It does separate cards (0.459) from non-cards (0.966), so Foilcase uses it only as a 0.80 ceiling.How do you read small printed numbers with VNRecognizeTextRequest?Lower , turn off language correction, never downscale the crop, and add passes over enlarged strips and corners. Rejecting blurry frames first matters most: it took collector-number accuracy from 32.8% to 78.5% in my harness.Why store a collection as an append-only ledger?Nothing is overwritten, so a mis-tap or a bug can't destroy data: removal is a tombstone and the item table is rebuilt from the log. Changed upstream ids resolve through an alias table at read time, without touching user data.

Most "find the best chess move" websites work the same way: you send a position to their server, an engine analyses it there, and they pay for every second of CPU. That's why so many of them cap free analysis per day or fill the page with ads.When I built Chess Move Finder, I went the other way. Stockfish 17.1 runs entirely in the user's browser. The server only serves static files. Analysis costs me nothing per user, so the tool can stay free with no account, no ads and no daily limit.Here's how it works and the tradeoffs I ran into. for the site, tools, blog and translated pagesStockfish 17.1, compiled to WebAssembly, using the  buildThe engine runs in a , so the board and UI never freeze while it thinksWhy the "lite single-threaded" buildStockfish's WebAssembly builds come in a few flavours, and the choice matters more than I expected.Multi-threaded builds need SharedArrayBuffer. That means the page must be : you have to send Cross-Origin-Opener-Policy and Cross-Origin-Embedder-Policy headers. That's fine for a single-page app, but it breaks a lot of normal things on a content site: third-party embeds, some analytics, images from other origins. My site has a blog and other tools alongside the engine, so I didn't want those headers everywhere.The single-threaded build needs none of that. It works on any page, in any modern browser, on any phone."Lite" means a smaller neural network (NNUE) file. The full network is large; the lite one is a fraction of the size. It plays slightly weaker, but it's still far stronger than any human, and the first analysis starts much faster, especially on mobile data. For a "what's the best move here?" tool, a fast first answer matters more than the last few Elo points.Talking to the engine: UCI over postMessageStockfish speaks  (Universal Chess Interface), a plain-text protocol. Inside a Web Worker, you send it strings and listen for strings back:const engine = new Worker('/engine/stockfish.js');

engine.onmessage = (e) => {
  const line = e.data;
  if (line.startsWith('info') && line.includes(' pv ')) {
    // depth, score (cp or mate), and the principal variation
  }
  if (line.startsWith('bestmove')) {
    const move = line.split(' ')[1]; // e.g. "e2e4"
  }
};

engine.postMessage('uci');
engine.postMessage('setoption name MultiPV value 3'); // top 3 lines
engine.postMessage('position fen ' + fen);
engine.postMessage('go movetime 1500');
A few things worth getting right: before sending a position, especially on slow devices where the WASM takes a moment to initialise.Always send  before a new search. Users click fast. Without it, results from an old position can arrive after you've shown a new one. gives the top three lines in one search. That powers the "compare the top lines" view, so users can see  the best move beats the alternatives.Quick vs Deep: time-boxed searchRather than searching to a fixed depth, the tool uses a :Quick: about 1.5 seconds. Enough for most positions on most devices. For sharp tactical positions where a longer search changes the answer.Fixed depth sounds cleaner, but depth 20 takes wildly different times on a desktop and a budget phone. A time budget keeps the experience predictable, and the engine simply reaches a deeper depth on faster hardware.Making engine output readableRaw UCI output isn't something most chess players want to read. The UI turns it into: for the best move: positive favours White, negative favours Black, and  means a forced mate is on the board, written as normal chess movesThe UCI score is always from the side to move's perspective, so you have to flip the sign when it's Black to move to show a consistent White-relative evaluation. It's a classic first bug.Most users arrive with a position from somewhere else: a game on Chess.com or Lichess, a puzzle, a book. So the tool accepts a  and also lets you set up the board by hand. Every position can also be , so you can send it to a friend and ask "what would you play here?"Once the engine was running client-side, adding more tools on top of it was cheap: from a PGN, plus practice from your own mistakes from the Lichess open puzzle database, filterable by difficulty and themeAn  for 20 openingsPlay against the computer at several levelsAll of it reuses the same in-browser engine.Weaker devices give shallower analysis in the same time. For a move-finder that's acceptable; for deep correspondence analysis it wouldn't be.First load downloads the engine and network. The lite build keeps that reasonable, and the browser caches it afterwards.No server means no central analysis cache. Every user computes their own positions. That's fine at this scale, and it's also why there's nothing to rate-limit.If you play chess (or just want to see Stockfish running in a tab), give it a go: . Paste a FEN, pick who moves, and see what Stockfish thinks.I'd love feedback, especially from anyone who has shipped WebAssembly engines in production. What would you have done differently?

Adding a new project to my portfolio takes 30 seconds. No component changes. No new routes. No layout adjustments. Just 4 lines of JSON and an image dropped into a folder.I got tired of the typical portfolio maintenance cycle: build something cool, then spend an hour wiring it into your portfolio site, adjusting layouts, making sure the new card doesn't break the grid. So I designed mine to be completely data-driven from the start.The Single Source of TruthEvery project on my portfolio lives in one file: . Each project is an object with exactly 4 fields:{
  id: 'crystal-index',
  title: 'Crystal Index',
  techStack: 'TypeScript, Next.js, Prisma, SQL, GPT4, React 3 Fiber',
  info: 'Custom CMS for cataloguing crystals with structured filters for colour, chakra, and properties, and GPT-4-generated descriptions.',
}
That's it. Four lines. The entire portfolio renders from an array of these objects.The  field is where the design gets interesting. It's not just an identifier. It serves three purposes simultaneously: The portfolio constructs repository URLs by appending the ID to a base GitHub URL.  becomes https://github.com/sammii-hk/crystal-index. No separate URL field needed. An auto-generated image map resolves the ID to the correct image file and extension.  maps to /assets/images/crystal-index.jpg. No manual image path configuration. The ID serves as the unique key when mapping over the array. Standard React pattern, but it comes free because the ID already exists for the other two purposes.One field, three jobs. This means there's no redundant data, no fields that can fall out of sync, and no opportunity for a GitHub link to point to the wrong repo because the image and the link derive from the same source.For projects under a GitHub organisation, the ID includes the org path: . The image utility handles this by splitting on  and taking the last segment for the filename lookup, while the full path constructs the correct GitHub URL.I didn't want to manually track whether each project screenshot is a  or . So I wrote a build script that scans the images directory and generates a JSON map:import { readdir, writeFile } from 'fs/promises';
import { join } from 'path';

const imagesDir = join(process.cwd(), 'public', 'assets', 'images');

async function generateImageMap() {
  const files = await readdir(imagesDir);
  const imageMap = {};

  files.forEach(file => {
    if (file === 'sammii.png') return;
    const name = file.replace(/\.(jpg|png)$/, '');
    const ext = file.endsWith('.png') ? 'png' : 'jpg';
    if (!imageMap[name]) imageMap[name] = ext;
  });

  const outputPath = join(process.cwd(), 'app', 'common', 'utils', 'image-map.json');
  await writeFile(outputPath, JSON.stringify(imageMap, null, 2));
}

generateImageMap();
The output is a simple lookup:{
  "crystal-index": "jpg",
  "lunary": "png",
  "succulent": "png",
  "day-lite": "jpg"
}
Then a utility function resolves any project ID to its full image path:import imageMapData from './image-map.json';
const imageMap = imageMapData;

export const getImagePath = (projectId) => {
  const projectBaseId = projectId.split('/').pop() || projectId;
  const extension = imageMap[projectBaseId] || 'jpg';
  return `/assets/images/${projectBaseId}.${extension}`;
};
Drop an image in the folder, run the script, done. The portfolio picks it up automatically.The portfolio has two completely different view modes: a responsive grid with click-to-expand modals, and a full-screen vertical carousel. Both consume the exact same projects array.The grid view maps over the array and renders cards:{projects.map((project) => (
  <div key={project.id} onClick={() => setSelectedProject(project)}>
    <ProjectItem project={project} isGrid={true} />
  </div>
))}
The carousel view maps over the same array and renders full-width slides:<Carousel
  items={projects}
  renderItem={({ item, isSnapPoint }) => (
    <CarouselItem key={item.id} isSnapPoint={isSnapPoint}>
      <ProjectItem project={item} />
    </CarouselItem>
  )}
/>
 accepts an  prop that switches between compact card layout and expanded layout. Same component, same data, two presentations. Adding a project to the array means it automatically appears in both views with no additional work.When I finish a new project, here's what I actually do:Drop it in  as Run node scripts/generate-image-map.mjsAdd 4 lines to The portfolio rebuilds and the new project appears in both views, with the correct image, the correct GitHub link, and the correct layout. Five steps, thirty seconds, zero component files touched.This isn't just about saving time on a portfolio. It's a pattern I use everywhere.My astrology app Lunary has a grimoire with over 2,000 articles. They're stored as structured data and rendered through shared components. Adding a new article about a crystal or a tarot card doesn't require touching any UI code.My publishing tool Spellcast manages multiple social media accounts and platforms. Account configurations are data objects. Adding a new platform means adding to the config, not rebuilding the interface.The principle is always the same: separate data from presentation. Make the data structure do the heavy lifting. Keep the components generic enough that they never need to know about specific content.If you're spending more time wiring new content into your portfolio than building the projects themselves, you've got the architecture backwards. Make your portfolio work for you, not the other way around.

A few weeks ago, I caught myself spending 40 minutes writing a form validation schema I had written a dozen times before. I wasn’t learning anything. I was just typing.That was the moment I decided to run an experiment: for 30 days, I would use AI coding assistants on every project I touched, and I would log what saved me time and what quietly wasted it.I expected a magic productivity boost. What I got was more nuanced. Some habits made me noticeably faster. Others made me slower while feeling productive, which is the worst combination.This is the honest breakdown, including the mistakes.I mostly build web apps with React and Node.js, with the occasional Python script for automation. During the experiment, I worked on three things:A client dashboard (React + Express)An internal automation script (Python)An older codebase I joined mid-month and had to understand quicklyI used ChatGPT, Claude, and GitHub Copilot alongside VS Code. To keep myself honest, I kept a plain notes file with three columns: the task, my estimate without help, and the actual time taken. It isn’t scientific, but it was enough to spot patterns.1. Handing off the boring parts firstThe biggest win was boilerplate: config files, validation, CRUD endpoints, regex, date formatting. I know exactly what I want in these tasks, but typing them out is tedious.Here is a real example. I needed to validate a signup request. Writing it by hand meant checking the docs for the right syntax, handling each field and then writing error messages. About 15 minutes.“Express 4, Node 20. Write a validation middleware for a signup route using Joi. Fields: name (2-50 chars), email, password (min 8, at least one number). Return 400 with a clear message for each failure.”I got a working first draft in under a minute, reviewed it, tweaked two messages and moved on. Total: about 3 minutes.const Joi = require('joi');
const signupSchema = Joi.object({
 name: Joi.string().min(2).max(50).required(),
 email: Joi.string().email().required(),
 password: Joi.string().min(8).pattern(/\d/).required()
});
function validateSignup(req, res, next) {
 const { error } = signupSchema.validate(req.body, { abortEarly: false });
 if (error) {
 return res.status(400).json({
 errors: error.details.map(d => d.message)
 });
 }
 next();
}
The rule I settled on: if I can explain the task in two sentences and I’d be bored writing it, hand it off.2. Writing prompts like bug reportsMy early prompts were lazy: “fix this error.” The answers were generic and often wrong.Then I started including four things every time:The language, framework and versionWhat I expected to happenWhat actually happened, with the exact error messageOnly the relevant code, not the whole fileCompare these two prompts:“I’m using Express 4 with Node 20. This POST route returns a 500 when the body is empty. Here’s the handler and the error log. I expect a 400 with a validation message.”The second one gets a useful answer on the first try almost every time. Good prompting turned out to be the same skill as writing a good bug report, and that skill makes you a better teammate even without any tools.3. Understanding code I didn’t writeThis surprised me the most. When I joined the older codebase, I pasted unfamiliar functions and asked, “Explain what this does, what its inputs are and what could break it.”I still read the code myself afterwards, but having a first explanation to check against cut my onboarding time from what I estimated would be a week to about three days. It also helped me find a function that silently swallowed errors, which nobody on the team knew about.4. Finding edge cases I would have missedI’m not great at imagining how my own code will fail. Asking for “10 edge cases for this function that might break it” gave me inputs I wouldn’t have tried: empty arrays, null values, unicode characters, extremely large numbers, duplicate entries.Three of those edge cases exposed real bugs in a date-range function I thought was finished.5. Rubber duck debugging, upgradedExplaining a bug to someone often solves it. These tools work like a duck that talks back. Half the time, I found the answer while typing out the question. The other half, the reply pointed me in the right direction.1. Trusting output without reading itIn week two, I accepted a generated function without a careful review. It looked clean and passed my quick test. It also had an off-by-one error that affected pagination, and it took me almost an hour to track down two days later.Generated code sounds confident even when it’s wrong. Now I treat it like a pull request from a junior developer: useful, but it always needs review.2. Asking for a whole feature at onceWhen I asked for an entire module in one prompt, the result was messy, inconsistent and hard to modify. Naming didn’t match my project, error handling was different in every file and some parts duplicated logic that already existed.Smaller pieces worked far better: one function, one component, one query at a time.3. Skipping the thinking stepThis was the hidden cost. When I jumped straight to prompting, I skipped the design thinking. I ended up with code that worked but didn’t fit the rest of the system, and I paid for it later during refactoring.Now I spend five minutes writing the approach in plain text before opening any tool: what the inputs are, what the output is, where the code will live. It saves far more time than it costs.4. Using it for things I was trying to learnWhen I was learning a new concept (database indexing, in my case), copying answers meant I didn’t actually learn anything. A week later, I couldn’t reproduce it without help.For learning, I now ask for hints and explanations, then try to write the solution myself first.5. Outdated or invented methodsA few times, I got suggestions that used library methods which don’t exist or were removed in newer versions. The code looked perfectly reasonable. It simply failed at runtime.Anything version-sensitive now gets checked against the official documentation.Here is the rough summary from my log:A net gain of roughly 14 hours over a month. That is real, but it’s much smaller than the hype suggests, and it only came from using the tools deliberately, not constantly. The weeks where I used them for everything were not my most productive weeks.After 30 days, this is the routine that stuck: Write the approach in a few lines, without any tool.Delegate the boring parts. Boilerplate, tests, repetitive transformations. Stack, version, expected result, actual result, error. One function at a time. Read it, run it, test it. whenever versions or APIs matter.Advice for Beginners vs Experienced DevelopersIf you’re just starting out, be careful. These tools can make you feel like you understand something when you don’t. Use them to explain, to give hints and to review your code, not to skip the struggle that builds skill.If you’re experienced, the value is in removing friction: the repetitive work, the first draft, the edge-case checklist. Your judgment is still the part that matters, and no tool replaces it.These tools don’t replace understanding. They reward developers who already know what they want and can judge the result.The people getting the most out of them aren’t the ones who use them the most. They’re the ones who know exactly when to use them, and when to close the tab and think.If you try your own 30-day experiment, I’d love to hear what you find. What worked for you, and what quietly wasted your time?
